  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[-1,1]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,1],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-1}^{1}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nCalculate the norm of the vector $f(t)=1-t.$",
  "worked_solution": "The norm of a vector $f(t)$ with respect to an inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\u2016f(t)\u2016=\\sqrt{\u27e8f(t),f(t)\u27e9}.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product using our definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u27e8f(t),f(t)\u27e9 & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(f(t))^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(1-t)^{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}({t}^{2}-2t+1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}-{t}^{2}+t)|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-1+1)-(\\frac{(-1)^{3}}{3}-(-1)^{2}+(-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\u2016f(t)\u2016=\\sqrt{\\frac{8}{3}}=2\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\n$$",
